      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by finding her walking rate in miles per minute. Given that Jana can walk 1 mile in 24 minutes, her rate is \\(\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\\).\n\nNext, we need to calculate the distance she covers in 10 minutes. To do this, we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values into this equation:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\left(\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify, we can rewrite \\(10\\) minutes as \\(\\frac{10}{1}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the decimal equivalent of this fraction. First, we divide 10 by 24:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = 0.416666 \\ldots\n\\]\n\nAfter simplifying, we can write this as:\n\n\\[\n0.416666 \\ldots\n\\]\n\nTo express one more decimal place for precision, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n0.416666 \\ldots \\times 0.1 = 0.041666 \\ldots \\ldots\n\\]\n\nThus, rounding to the nearest tenth, we have:\n\n\\[\n0.4\n\\]\n\nSo, Jana will walk \\(\\boxed{0.4}\\) miles in 10 minutes.\n\n200 actors are present in a movie theater. 40% of them are women. The remaining fraction present among the actors are men. Among the men in the theater, half of them are wearing glasses and 20% of them are indigenous. If 10% of the women in the theater are wearing glasses and 50% of them are indigenous, how many indigenous men are present in the theater? Additionally, how many indigenous women are present in the theater?\n\nFirst, let's calculate the number of women and men in the theater.\nThe number of women is $\\frac{40}{100} \\times 200 = 80$.\nThe number of men is $200 - 80 = 120$.\nHalf of the men in the theater are wearing glasses, so the number of men wearing glasses is $\\frac{120}{2} = 60$.\n20% of the men are indigenous, so the number of indigenous men is $\\frac{20}{100} \\times 120 = 24$.\n10% of the women in the theater are wearing glasses, so the number of women wearing glasses is $\\frac{10}{100} \\times 80 = 8$.\n50% of the women are indigenous, so the number of indigenous women is $\\frac{50}{100} \\times 80 = 40$.\nThe number of indigenous men is $\\boxed{24}$.\nThe number of indigenous women is $\\boxed{40}$.\nThis solution is correct. Let's verify it using the given conditions by calculating the number of indigenous individuals in different categories (i.e., men and women) in the theater within our given assumptions.\nGiven that the men in the theater belong to the same category which is indistinguishable from that of women, we can find the number of native men and women in the theater using the remaining numbers not accounted for by glasses/washing and wearing glasses given, after accounting for the 10% of each group wearing glasses and washing glasses.\n\nThus, using the equation derived from the problem:\n\\begin{align*}\n \\text{Indigenous women} &= 0.5 \\times 200 - \\left[0.1 \\times 200 + 0.5 \\times 200 \\times 0.2\\right] \\\\\n &\\above\\left\\&\\left[\\(Actors in the theater - Indigenous women =\\)(\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\\end{align*}",
