      "text": "To find out how far Jana walks in one mile in 10 minutes, we first need to determine her walking speed. \n\n1. We know that Jana walks a mile in 24 minutes. This means her walking speed is \\(\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\\).\n\n2. To find out how far she walks in 10 minutes, we can use the formula distance = speed × time.\n\n3. Substituting the value we have for speed (which is \\(\\frac{1}{24}\\) mile per minute) and the given time (10 minutes) into the formula, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = \\left( \\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute} \\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n4. Simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\) to its lowest terms. Both 10 and 24 can be divided by 2:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12} \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n5. Convert the fraction \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) to a decimal:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.4167\n   \\]\n\n6. Round 0.4167 to the nearest tenth:\n   \\[\n   0.4167 \\text{ rounded to the nearest tenth is } 0.4\n   \\]\n\nThus, Jana will walk approximately 0.4 miles in 10 minutes. The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
